Depending a *lot* on the opponent.  While I cannot compare with a chess 
master, I think that I'd beat more than 80% of randomly chosen human 
opponents at chess.  But I am pretty sure that I'll not beat any one of 
the newer chess computers.  I have an old one (from 1985) that I can 
still beat on intermediate difficulty levels.  However, chess has become 
much less fun since computers beat you all the time, unless you are a 
grand master.  So I think chess is difficult nowadays not because of the 
human opponent but because computers play much better than most humans.

The difference with nethack is, of course, that you are not really 
playing *against* the computer.  Neither against the RNG, which is not 
interested in your winning or losing.  The computer just produces the 
playground - it is the dungeon master.

